  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nToday through next week (Friday)...\n\nRest of today through this weekend:\n\nMorning synoptic/RAP analysis indicates ridge centered just off the \nFL Peninsula in the western Atlantic, while another ridge is\ncentered over the Four Corners. The Gulf Coast region remains on \nthe western periphery of the former mentioned ridge. High moisture\nladen atmospheric profiles (around 2 inches) and a stationary \nfrontal boundary are situated across the region. This is keeping \nconvection initiation (CI) slow to develop across the region. \nExpect medium to high rain chances to become scattered to numerous\n(40 to 70 percent) while high rain chances and widespread \ncoverage in the Pine Belt (greater than 70 to 80 percent \ncoverage). While there could be some storms across the area, weak \nskinny thermo profiles and meager lapse rates will limit \nconvective organization. HREF PMMs indicate some low chance probs \n(around 10 percent) of exceeding 3 inch rainfall totals. However, \nwith confidence on the low side, not advertising anything in the \nHWO graphics. Temperatures will be seasonably warm today, some \n5-10F above (86-91F). \n\nRain chances decrease in coverage into early evening, with \nadditional development overnight, but medium to high chance and \nscattered to numerous coverage most likely in the Pine Belt through \ndaybreak Sunday. Lows will seasonably warm, some 8-15F above (65-\n71F). Deep moisture will persist into Sunday, with stationary \nboundary locked in along the Natchez Trace corridor to just a touch \nsoutheast. While there could be some locally heavy downpours again, \ndiurnal heating will be even less than today and should limit \nconvection intensity and rainfall totals overall. There are some \nHREF/REFS low chance probs (around 10 percent) of 24 hour rainfall \ntotals exceeding 3 inches in the Highway 98 to Interstate 59 \ncorridors. /DC/\n\nNext week: As the front begins to make headway into the area Monday, \nrain will come to an end as dry air pushes in its wake. 50s \nDewpoints in the upper will allow lows to cool into the middle 50s \nin the north and low 60s in the south. There is a bit of crossover \nMonday night, especially in the southeast, so fog/low stratus is a \npossibility.\n\nThe remainder of the week will be dry, reinforced by NW flow in the \nwake of the trough. Temperatures will be more seasonal with highs in \nthe 80s and lows in the 60s. Rain chances return next weekend as a \nshortwave out of Texas/western Gulf will return the moist airmass \nwith 2+ inch PWAT. Although there is some flow with this shortwave, \nlapse rates are rather moist adiabatic, thus insufficient for \nsevere. There may be enough support however to necessitate \nmonitoring for heavy rainfall as we get closer. /SAS/",
